@meta
id: 06-18
title: Surgery on a dense provider
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: find-f, find-t, repeat-semicolon, line-end
par: 104
minutes: 9
vscode: nine clicks, and you will misplace two of them
open: refund.provider.ts errors.json

@brief
The hardest line in this chapter is the third one, so read the whole job
first.

  1. The signature has lost all three spaces after its colons, and `reason`
     is optional: `(orderId: string, amount: number, reason?: string)`.

  2. The `findOne` call must also load the customer, and it is not
     terminated. It should read:

         findOne({ where: { id: orderId }, include: { customer: true } });

     Note where the new text goes: directly after the inner `}` that closes
     `{ id: orderId }`, and before the space in front of the outer `}`. The
     line ends with two closing braces, so the brace you are aiming at is
     not the nearest one.

  3. The refund call passes the reason through, and is terminated:

         refund(order.id, amount, reason);

`errors.json` is open for reference and must be left exactly as it is. With
two files open, vim refuses the first `:wq` with `E173: 1 more file to edit`;
repeat it and it quits.

Counts work on `t` and `T` the same way they work on `f` and `F`. That is
the key to edit 2.

@keys
| `f:` `;` | the first colon, then each one after it |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `2T}` | backward until just past the second `}` |
| `F)` | backward onto the closing paren |
| `i` `a` | insert before or after the cursor |

@hints
Take the signature with one `f:` and three `;` repeats. Two of those inserts
add a space after the colon, one adds a `?` before it — and after the `?`
the cursor is one character short of the colon it needs a space after, so a
`;` from there finds that same colon again. Use it.
--
On the `findOne` line, terminate it first with `A` `;` `Esc`. You are now at
the right-hand end with two braces to your left. The inner one is the second
brace going backwards, and you want to land just past it, which is a
backwards `t` with a count: `2T}`.
--
On the refund call, terminate it, then `F)` puts you on the closing paren
and `i` writes the new argument in front of it.

@solution
    j f : a  <Esc> ; a  <Esc> ; i ? <Esc> ; a  <Esc>
    j A ; <Esc> 2T} i , include: { customer: true } <Esc>
    2j A ; <Esc> F ) i , reason <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Around seventy-six keystrokes, fifty of which are the text you had to type
anyway. Everything else is travel, and no piece of travel was longer than
three keys.

The `2T}` is the lesson. Faced with two closing braces the untrained instinct
is to go to the end of the line and nudge left with `h` until it looks right,
counting characters by eye and getting it wrong when the formatting changes.
The trained instinct is to say what you mean: second brace, backwards, stop
just past it. It is one motion, it is exact, and it stays exact if someone
renames `orderId` to something twice as long.

The other thing to take away is the `;` after the `?`. You inserted before
the colon, so the cursor was still short of it, so a repeat of the find
landed on the same colon rather than the next one — which happened to be
precisely what you needed. Knowing where a find leaves the cursor is what
lets you chain these without looking, and you now know it for all four keys:
`f` and `F` land on, `t` and `T` land beside.

@start refund.provider.ts
export class RefundProvider {
  public async refund(orderId:string, amount:number, reason:string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: orderId } })

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount)
  }
}

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}

@expected refund.provider.ts
export class RefundProvider {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number, reason?: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: orderId }, include: { customer: true } });

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount, reason);
  }
}
